## Your rights at work

Under the Equality Act rules, **addiction to drugs is not counted as a disability in itself**, unless it started from medicine that was **prescribed** to you. But conditions **caused by or linked to** drug use, such as **depression, anxiety or physical health problems**, can count if they have a substantial, long-term effect on your daily life.

Many drug services offer **employment support** to help you find or stay in work.

## Driving

You must tell the **DVLA** if you have a drug dependence or misuse drugs. It is illegal to drive with certain drugs in your body, including some prescribed medicines above set limits. Check GOV.UK for the rules.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your health affects you on **most days**. It does not matter what caused your condition. If drug dependence, or health problems linked to it, mean you need help with things like **managing treatment, managing money, preparing food or getting around**, you may be entitled to PIP.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **[Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/)** or **[New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/)**. Free advisers, such as Citizens Advice, can help with benefits, debt and housing.
